5	
6	# Upstream note: MIDI out from UART1's transmit side, and the app's CoreMIDI output
7	
8	For the owner to apply in the F-Emulator app, and to offer to
9	`joakimeriksson/fm1-emu` if wanted. Spec side only; not filed by a
10	session. Written in session midi-in-out (2026-10-24) against commit
11	`43ed5580064885e93bc8d20be931ae950d10cd4f`; our copy carries the change as
12	`vendor/emulator/patches/0009-midi-out-transmit-side.patch` (fwtest 0.5.0).
13	docs/EMULATOR-PARITY.md lists it.
14	
15	## What upstream does
16	
17	UART1 (`0x12100`) models only the MIDI IN jack's receive DMA. A write of
18	`TXCNT` (`0x12118`) sets the transmit pending (`CON0` bit 15) at once and
19	sends nothing ("no TX pin on the board"); a `BUF` (`0x1210C`) write is
20	plain storage. So a firmware's MIDI out vanishes, and its transmit
21	interrupt comes the instant it starts a transfer, not 320 µs per byte
22	later, which hides pacing bugs.
23	
24	## What the patch does
25	
26	In `fm1-soc`'s `periph.rs` and `lib.rs`:
27	
28	- `TXCNT` written: `lib.rs` reads `TXCNT & 0xffff` bytes from `TXADR`
29	  (`0x12114`, kept in SFR storage) at that moment and hands them to
30	  `Periph::uart_transmit`. A `BUF` write hands over its low byte.
31	- `uart_transmit` drops them (counted in `midi_out_dropped()`) when
32	  `CON0` bit 0 is clear; otherwise it queues them behind anything still
33	  going. One byte goes per `midi_byte_time()` (10 bits at 31 250 baud:
34	  76 800 cycles at 240 MHz), whatever `BAUD` says, as the receive side
35	  does. `next_event` includes the transmitter, so the core never runs or
36	  skips past a byte's end.
37	- When the queue empties, `tpnd` is set and `uart_update` raises IRQ 20
38	  if `CON0` bit 2 is set; the existing `CON0` bit 13 write clears it.
39	- Every byte sent is kept as `(cycle its stop bit ended, byte)` until
40	  `Periph::midi_take_sent()` drains them, oldest first.
41	
42	The patch applies to upstream after fwtest's 0001 to 0008 with line
43	offsets only; it touches no instruction decoding. fwtest's harness test
44	`transmits_midi` and the suite case `midiout` check it; without the patch
45	the same image's transmit interrupt runs twice, at once on each DMA (0 and
46	4.0 ms), instead of three times as each transfer's last byte ends (1.600,
47	3.320 and 5.281 ms).
48	
49	## The app: sending the bytes to CoreMIDI
50	
51	The board each package runs on matters only for what the user is told:
52	the SMK-37 has a MIDI OUT jack these bytes model; a real FM-1 has none
53	(docs/HARDWARE-SPEC.md, HW-67, HW-72 to HW-74), so for an FM-1 package
54	this is the emulator's extra, and should say so in the UI.
55	
56	1. **A virtual source.** At start-up, `MIDIClientCreateWithBlock`, then
57	   `MIDISourceCreateWithProtocol` (`kMIDIProtocol_1_0`) named, say, "FM-1
58	   Emulator MIDI Out", so a DAW or another synth can pick it as an input.
59	   Give it a stable `kMIDIPropertyUniqueID` (saved in the app's
60	   preferences) so DAWs keep the routing across launches.
61	2. **Drain every frame.** After each slice of emulation, call
62	   `soc.periph.midi_take_sent()`. Never let the bytes pile up: the
63	   emulator keeps them only until taken.
64	3. **Bytes to messages.** CoreMIDI wants whole messages: parse the byte
65	   stream as MIDI 1.0 does, keeping the running status, passing real-time
66	   bytes (`F8` to `FF`) on at once even inside another message, and
67	   collecting system exclusive from `F0` to `F7` (a status byte other than
68	   real-time ends it unterminated: drop it). fwtest's decoder,
69	   `harness/src/midi.rs` (MIT, ours, no emulator code), does exactly this
70	   and can be ported; its tests list the edge cases. Expand running status
71	   into full messages before sending.
72	4. **Times.** Each byte's cycle is emulated time at 240 MHz. Keep one
73	   anchor, the host time (`mach_absolute_time()`) and emulated cycle when
74	   emulation (re)starts or the user changes speed; a message's host time is
75	   `anchor_host + (cycle - anchor_cycle) / 240e6` seconds, converted with
76	   `mach_timebase_info`, plus a fixed latency (say 5 ms) so messages
77	   scheduled from a frame already in the past still go out evenly. If
78	   emulation runs slower than real time, or a computed time is already
79	   past, send with timestamp 0 (now) rather than late-stamped.
80	5. **Send.** Build a `MIDIEventList` with `MIDIEventListInit` and
81	   `MIDIEventListAdd`, one MIDI 1.0 UMP per channel message (system
82	   exclusive as UMP data packets of up to six bytes each), and
83	   `MIDIReceivedEventList` on the virtual source. On macOS before 11,
84	   the `MIDIPacketList` and `MIDIReceived` calls do the same with the
85	   bytes as they are.
86	6. **Panic on stop.** When emulation stops, pauses, resets or loads
87	   another package mid-note, send note-off for every note the stream left
88	   on (track note-ons per channel and key as you parse), or `B0+n 7B 00`
89	   (all notes off) on each channel used, so nothing downstream hangs.
90	
91	## Checking it
92	
93	Run the harness test image `harness/tests/images/midiout.bin` in the app
94	with the output connected to a MIDI monitor (MIDI Monitor, or a DAW's MIDI
95	input view): two note-ons on channel 1 (60 and 62), a clock, a note-off
96	for 60 and an active-sensing byte, as `harness/tests/golden/midiout/
97	midi-out.txt` lists them, the five at 0.96, 1.6, 3.32, 4.96 and 5.28 ms
98	after start.
